          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Butter or curds most commonly signify rich nourishment, material abundance, prosperity, and divine provision, though context can instead suggest subsistence, hospitality, smooth deceptive speech, or the result of churning.\",\"book_summary\":\"Butter signifies teaching or the word processed into sound judgment and embodied in a cleansed walk, enabling moral discernment; false butter-like smoothness signifies speech concealing hostility.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Rich nourishment and material abundance associated with divine provision.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Processed teaching that matures into moral discernment and practical purity.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book reclassifies Isaiah 7 and Job 29 from food associated primarily with abundance or subsistence into stages or effects of spiritual instruction, extends the image to foot washing in John 13, and retains Psalm 55's established deceptive-speech usage. Proverbs 30's churning image is also redirected from a general process-and-result analogy to the processing of teaching.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"In the same symbolic role, the book replaces the consensus's central referent—material plenty and nourishing provision—with a distinct referent, tested teaching that produces judgment and cleansed conduct. These may coexist as literal and allegorical readings, but they are not equivalent core identifications.\",\"rationale\":\"The entry is not NOVEL because the consensus listed minority analogies involving spiritual nourishment, doctrinal richness, mature revelation, churning, and deceptive smooth speech. Nevertheless, those were peripheral possibilities, whereas the book makes processed teaching and discernment the governing identity and applies it across passages normally understood in terms of abundance or concrete food. This is replacement of the modal core rather than a mere narrowing of abundance.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Isaiah 7:15 explicitly links eating butter and honey with refusing evil and choosing good, while Proverbs 30:33 supplies the processing motif and Psalm 55:21 directly supports deceptive smooth speech. Thus the book has textual hooks, and the consensus itself anticipated doctrinal nourishment. However, the identification of butter specifically with processed teaching, the reading of Job 29 as cleansing one's walk, and especially the John 13 connection depend on allegorical linkage rather than explicit statements in the printed citations.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
